<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The vfu-doorbell rig on the xio guest: an overlay, not a fleet of its own.

  It adds rocm-xio's two tools/vfu-doorbell servers to the xio stack's
  devices. The guest then has, besides the xio devices, a ringee, a vfio-user
  PCI device whose BAR0 carries doorbell overlays (index, keyed, event) with
  readable per-key counters, and a ringer, one the guest programs to write a
  pattern of values to any guest physical address through vfu_mmio_write.

  It is an overlay on purpose. In xio.xml itself the two servers would be
  part of the default xio flavour, and that flavour would then depend on an
  image rocm-xio's CI has to have pushed.

  The servers run from an image, not from a binary on the host. rocm-xio's
  CI (.github/workflows/vfu-doorbell-image.yml) builds /opt/vfu/vfu-doorbell on
  the libvfio-user fork image and pushes it to ghcr.io, tagged with the commit
  and with the branch name. So there is nothing to build before bringing the
  stack up, and the first `up` pulls it.

  `qemu-tool quick-start -\-flavor xio -\-vfu-doorbell` layers this file onto
  the xio stack for you, and runs the guest half in the xio playbook. The file
  ships in the wheel and the .deb for that reason. To use it by hand instead,
  as a layer after xio.xml:

    qemu-tool gen-compose -\-xml qemu/stacks/xio.xml \
        -\-xml qemu/overlays/vfu-doorbell.xml \
        -\-output-dir /var/lib/qemu-tool/stacks/vfu-doorbell

  The ringer's writes into the ringee need a QEMU whose vfio_user_dma_write
  dispatches to MMIO regions. Whether the <qemu-ref> in xio.xml has that is
  not established here; if it has not, every ringer write fails with ENODEV.
-->
<fleet xmlns="http://qemu-tool/fleet/1.0">
  <!-- The servers' image, built by rocm-xio's CI from the branch of the
       same name as the tag. The tag moves with the branch. To run exactly the
       commit the guest installs, name the commit's tag here and the same
       40-character id in <xio-commit>. It is built on the libvfio-user fork
       image that carries vfu_mmio_write, vfu.3719c3a, the revision the QEMU
       in xio.xml is built against. The tag names no vfu.<sha>, so unlike
       <spdk-image> render time cannot hold it to QEMU's. -->
  <doorbell-image>ghcr.io/rocm/rocm-xio-vfu-doorbell:feat-vfu-doorbell</doorbell-image>

  <!-- The rocm-xio the guest installs: the branch that carries
       tools/vfu-doorbell and kernel/vfu-dma, in place of the role's own pin.
       No <xio-commit>, so it is built as pushed, like the image above. That
       is deliberate while the branch is still moving. -->
  <xio-ref>feat/vfu-doorbell</xio-ref>

  <devices>
    <!-- Appended after the xio devices, in this order: the ringee is the
         device being rung, the ringer the one doing the ringing. -->
    <server type='vfu-ringee' id='ringee'/>
    <server type='vfu-ringer' id='ringer'/>
  </devices>
</fleet>
